%S A399761 2,2,3,8,7,10,7,9,3,7,9,10,7,3,8,9,15,7,12,4,8,10,14,7,10,11,3,12,9,
%T A399761 17,13,7,9,9,10,15,11,16,8,16,12,12,19,5,8,15,9,14,11,10,15,15,15,16,
%U A399761 11,11,12,18,12,17,14,13,8,13,10,10,18,9,14,14,14,15,7
%N A399761 Number of steps starting from the n-th positive integer not divisible by 3 until the first repeated value under the reduced residue-dependent 4k + {1,2} map, or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A399761 a(n) is the number of iterations of T, starting from A001651(n), until a value occurs that has already appeared earlier in the same trajectory, where T(k) = (4*k+2)/3^v_3(4*k+2) if k == 1 (mod 3), and T(k) = (4*k+1)/3^v_3(4*k+1) if k == 2 (mod 3).
%C A399761 Here A001651(n) = floor((3*n-1)/2) is the n-th positive integer not divisible by 3, and v_3(m) = A007949(m).
%C A399761 The two cycles observed in the author's finite exhaustive computation are 1 -> 2 -> 1 and 7 -> 10 -> 14 -> 19 -> 26 -> 35 -> 47 -> 7.
%C A399761 If a trajectory has preperiod length mu and eventual cycle length lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda. Hence a starting value on the two-cycle has value 2, while a starting value on the seven-cycle has value 7.
%C A399761 The map differs from the residue-dependent 4k - {1,2} map in A399120 by the signs of the residue-dependent constants.
%H A399761 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="/A399761/b399761.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399761 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22823314">A Ternary (4k+1(2))/3 Collatz-Type Map with Two Attracting Cycles: Theoretical Analysis and Exhaustive Computational Verification up to 10^11</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A399761 Let b(n) = A001651(n) = floor((3*n-1)/2).
%F A399761 a(n) = min { j >= 1 : T^j(b(n)) = T^i(b(n)) for some 0 <= i < j }, if such j exists; otherwise a(n) = -1.
%F A399761 Equivalently, if the trajectory of b(n) has preperiod length mu and eventual period lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%e A399761 For n = 1, b(1) = 1 and the orbit begins 1, 2, 1. The first repeated value occurs after 2 iterations, so a(1) = 2.
%e A399761 For n = 3, b(3) = 4 and the orbit begins 4, 2, 1, 2. The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(3) = 3.
%e A399761 For n = 4, b(4) = 5 and the orbit begins 5, 7, 10, 14, 19, 26, 35, 47, 7. The first repeated value occurs after 8 iterations, so a(4) = 8.
%e A399761 For n = 5, b(5) = 7 and the orbit begins 7, 10, 14, 19, 26, 35, 47, 7. The first repeated value occurs after 7 iterations, so a(5) = 7.
%o A399761 (Python)
%o A399761 def T(k):
%o A399761     y = 4*k + (2 if k % 3 == 1 else 1)
%o A399761     while y % 3 == 0:
%o A399761         y //= 3
%o A399761     return y
%o A399761 def a(n):
%o A399761     k, seen = (3*n - 1)//2, set()
%o A399761     while k not in seen:
%o A399761         seen.add(k)
%o A399761         k = T(k)
%o A399761     return len(seen)
%o A399761 # This program assumes that the trajectory eventually repeats; it does not decide divergence.
%Y A399761 Cf. A001651, A007949, A399120.
%K A399761 nonn,new
%O A399761 1,1
%A A399761 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Oct 16 2026
